hi there Steve Kaufman here um I'm  leaving tomorrow for China today I had a  easy day here in Vancouver the weather  was it did rain but it was overcast  while I prepared myself and and you know  I was thinking about something that has  come up partly as a result of what we're  doing at link and that is the degree to  which language learning needs to be an  interactive learning process and as many  of you know I stayed here what I like to  do and number of people disagree some  come on and comment and disagree some  people just give me the thumbs down  that's fine but I still like to stay you  know what what I believe in so far as  language learning is concerned and I one  of the areas where I think I disagree  the most with even the more progressive  parts of mainstream language teaching is  in this need for sort of interactivity  in the early stages of learning an  example what we do at link is is very  close to the theories of TP rx which is  teaching proficiency through reading and  storytelling very much input based I  agree with it I think it's great and I  don't know much beyond what i have read  on the internet about it but it seems to  you know except the fact that people  will learn from stories from listening  from lots of reading but the  practitioners of tprs like to prepare  stories where a very limited number of  words and phrases are repeated maybe 50  times in the story in the hope that then  those words and phrases will really be  absorbed by the learner personally I  find myself resisting that I think a  story that is construed to have that  same or this limited number of words and  phrases appear so often will be  uninteresting  I don't think that however often I hear  certain words and phrases that I will  necessarily nail them down I know that  in my check learning certain words you  know not necessarily obscure but words  that mean you know like I think of pune  budge or protege which means because but  other words like Kanye coud comes up all  the time I look it up I still don't know  what it means I must have looked it up  10 times it's come up all the time I  looked it up i forget it so you know  repetition is not going to ensure it's  like I always say you can't nail things  down one day for whatever reason that  word will fall into place and i'll be  able to use it in the meantime every  time i come across it i have to look it  up even though i've come across at many  many times other words i see them once i  remember so you know this also came up  we have started at link offering some  lessons from lingua phone integrated  with the link system that caused a  little bit of a controversy at link  because the lingua phone style is very  different from our more passive input  based learning style at link but you  know there are different kinds of  learners but one of the things that they  like to do is have the learner even in  the beginning lessons sort of listen and  repeat and much more sort of complete a  word so if the word is señorita they  will say send yo D and you have to go  senorita so again it's an effort to  engage the learner in doing something  pimpster does this as well Tim star asks  you questions prompts you to answer  questions most language learning systems  seem to do this they ask you to you no  answer your question about the text that  you have heard or change the tents of  something constantly asking you to do  something I don't like doing those  things I think they slow you down and  that's perhaps where  I'm interested in feedback I mean to me  the task is to acquire words and and and  to immerse yourself in the language and  the minute I'm asked to change the tents  of something in a sentence or two you  know remember whether the lady you know  what she bought at the store or any such  comprehension questions that just throws  me off my track if I take my own example  now with check which which I started  from scratch at link here i am with less  than three months under my belt and I'm  picking my way through a book that was  written you know in the 1920s or  whenever it was written the good soldier  schwake and I'm loving it because it  brings me back into that period of the  austro-hungarian empire on the evolving  during the First World War and I  occasionally go to to look up certain  things relating to that period in  history I'm not at the point where i  have another book are unchecked history  which i'm reading away from the computer  so i can't import it into link  unfortunately there's lots of words i  don't know but i can still follow it so  I'm just passively ingesting the  language and tremendously enjoyable when  I go to speak let's say in three or four  months from now I will have a tremendous  vocabulary at my disposal I don't need  seems to me in Lesson one to repeat you  know proto j.j protégé or any such you  know word I don't need to be prompted to  start using the language in an  artificial way when I go to use it in a  natural and meaningful way I will  struggle and and so the same also  applies to you know this shadowing which  is and you know there are people who  swear by this and say it's very  effective so I'm not saying that it  isn't I'm just saying that I don't find  it to be the case I have tried repeating  after or even some people like to read  out loud memorize and these are all  things that I I just can't continue  doing I do a little  of them just to try them out doesn't  seem to have much effect and I just  revert back to my passive call it  passive learning but when we are  learning passively through listening and  reading and reviewing words and phrases  this passive learning is a form of  interaction we are interacting with the  language we're interacting with words or  interacting with meaning we're entering  this other language world creating  associations surely that's interactive  I'm not sure it's less meaningful  interaction than being forced to you no  answer something on cue or finish a word  or any of those other things that are  you know an attempt to engage us before  we're ready to be engaged and I also as  I said earlier I don't think that all of  that activity is going to enable you to  nail down any vocabulary because if if  you are doing a story and it has three  words and three phrases repeated 50  times and at the end of a week even if  you could match to those three words in  three phases it's only three words and  three phrases besides I said all that  you can match them if I look at at my  own results in check with less than  three months under my belt I have many  many thousands of words now in my  vocabulary if I have tried to nail every  one of those down at an earlier stage I  think the number of words in my  vocabulary today would number in the  hundreds so there you have it to one  extent and how useful is it to try to  create this interactivity to engage the  learner to force the learner to produce  something to say something to repeat  something is it necessary what are the  pedagogical reasons for it am I  completely wrong and suggesting that  there is another approach which is  easier less stressful and at least in my  case I think leads to better results so  look forward to hearing your comments  bye for now now I my most I can't seem  to  try not to swear why this is going on  here because my mouse wanders into two  screens and I had it not lined up and  here we go so yeah thanks for listening  and for computers
 

